  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x$ is not a solution.",
